  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 254 AM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging will begin building into the region through the \nday today as an upper level low over CO and an open wave across SK \nboth continue eastward. Lingering low-level moisture across SW \nvalleys is resulting in a mix of low clouds and fog early this \nmorning. The fog and low clouds look to slowly diminish through the \nday. Temperatures will remain on the cooler side in these area, \nparticularly where a considerable snowpack remains. \n\nTemperatures trend warmer toward the end of the week as upper \nridging remains in place. A fast moving shortwave flattens the ridge \nSaturday afternoon into the evening, which is a favorable pattern for \ngusty winds across the region. This time is no exception, with \nforecast soundings in the Cut Bank and Browning areas showing gusts \nin excess of 50 mph by Saturday afternoon. Trends will be monitored \nhere with respect to the timing and location of the fast moving \nshortwave. A slower moving wave would be less favorable for the \nstrongest gusts being realized at the surface while a faster moving \nshortwave would be more conducive to more widespread stronger gusts. \nAdditionally, there are some guidance members that take the bulk of \nthe shortwave north of the International Border which would trend \nwinds down if that scenario were to be realized. \n\nThe aforementioned shortwave departs Sunday morning, with transient \nupper level ridging quickly building back in. This ridging does not \nlast long however, as large scale upper level troughing is strongly \nfavored to move on shore across the west coast Monday. There is \nconsiderable uncertainty with respect to the track it will take, \nhowever. The main takeaway at this point is for at least a low chance \nfor precipitation returning by Monday night. That said, there will \nbe a bit of volatility in the forecast toward the middle of next \nweek until confidence builds in how the system will progress. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds Saturday:\n\nProbability for 58 mph wind gusts has trended upward by about 10% \nfor Cut Bank and 20% for Browning over the past 24 hours or so (to \n25% and 55% respectively). While confidence is increasing in the \nstronger winds, there remains some timing and location differences \nin the system responsible for the gusts that will strongly play into \njust how strong the winds get. \n\nTroughing next week:\n\nCluster guidance is essentially unanimous in upper level troughing \nmoving in across the west coast early next week. Confidence rapidly \nwanes after that however, with guidance showing many potential \nscenarios. Most show a cutoff low somewhere near the Southwest US, \nthough its exact location will have significant play into whether \nthe area sees precipitation or not. Additionally, several \ndeterministic models send additional shortwaves in from the Pacific \nNW and vicinity at the same time that the clusters are not able to \nresolve. This further adds to the complexity of the forecast. As \nmentioned prior, the main takeaway for this timeframe is for at \nleast a low chance for precipitation. Confidence is far too low to \nsay anything more than that at this time. -AM",
